| RR     | 100 ms  | 517711350 ns      | 1785833118 ns        | 52              |

## Conclusion

RR outperformed EEVDF in all measured tests. These workloads are CPU-bound, so lower context-switch overhead has a strong effect on average waiting time and completion time. The averages also combine long and short tasks, which can hide the response delay experienced by short tasks.

For RR, a longer quantum, such as 100 ms, produces fewer context switches therefore achieved the best average results in these tests. However the tradeoff is responsiveness as a new runnable task may have to wait longer before receiving CPU time. In the other hand, a shorter quantum produces more context switches and overhead, but gives runnable tasks more frequent opportunities to execute.

In theory, EEVDF is more suitable for interactive or mixed workloads because it uses a shorter time slice (4 ms in this implementation) and considers runtime and lag when selecting tasks. This can improve fairness and response time, although it may increase context-switch overhead.